          equipment: 'Standard 52-card pack with one Queen removed, or a dedicated Old Maid deck.',
          dealing: "Deal all cards out one by one. It doesn't matter if some players have one more card than others.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Pair up and discard all your cards; the player left holding the single remaining Queen (the Old Maid) at the end loses.',
          gameplay: 'The dealer deals all 51 cards one by one; some players may have one more card. Everyone discards any pairs (and from triplets or quartets, discard two or two pairs). The dealer offers their hand face down to the player on their left, who draws one card without looking. If it makes a pair they discard the pair. The player who drew then offers their hand to the next player left; play continues clockwise. Players drop out when their hand is empty. The player left holding the single Queen (the Old Maid) loses.',
          keyRules: [
            'Pairs are two cards of equal rank (e.g. two sevens or two kings).',
            'The player who just drew a card offers their hand to the next player to their left.',
            'If you empty your hand you are safe and take no further part; turn passes to the next player.',
            'The holder of the single remaining Queen (Old Maid) at the end loses.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Keep your cards spread out! If you are holding the Old Maid, try to keep your face neutral. If the player drawing from you sees you smiling or looking nervous when they touch a certain card, they will avoid it. Conversely, if you want them to pick it, try to hide it slightly higher or lower in your hand so it 'sticks out' naturally.",
          intermediate: "The 'Card Shuffle': When it's your turn to have a card drawn, shuffle your hand behind your back or under the table. This prevents the drawing player from tracking where you moved the Old Maid from the previous round.",
          advanced: "Bluff-Targeting: If you have two cards and one is the Old Maid, you can try to trick the opponent by holding the 'safe' card slightly forward. Many children reflexively pick the 'easiest' card to grab. If you know an opponent uses this habit, place the Old Maid in the prominent position.",
          tips: [
            "In a specialized deck, the Old Maid often has a distinct character (the 'Nanny' or 'Aunt'). Children often focus on the art, which makes bluffing harder!",
            'If you draw a card that completes a pair, discard it IMMEDIATELY to reduce your hand size and improve your odds of not having the Old Maid drawn back from you.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
